WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.430
They say "You've got to spend money to make
money," and plenty of major movies have translated

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:07.040
huge budgets into even greater returns.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.650
But sometimes, pricey, high-profile projects
just don't pay off.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:14.550
Here's a look at some big-budget films that
were almost totally rejected by North American

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:15.940
audiences.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:17.160
Cutthroat Island

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.910
Making a pirate movie that looks good is always
going to be expensive — the details don't

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:22.970
come cheap, and neither does top talent.

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:28.050
For the sweeping 1995 pirate adventure Cutthroat
Island, Carolco pictures tapped Renny Harlin,

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:32.590
a proven action director who'd just helmed
two hits: Die Hard 2 and Cliffhanger.

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:36.080
Harlin's pick to star: Academy Award winner
Geena Davis, who was also his wife at the

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:36.940
time.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:39.880
"Bad dog!"

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:50.820
But Davis wasn't a proven action star, and
pirate movies hadn't been successful in Hollywood

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:51.820
for decades.

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:54.210
This was pre-Pirates of the Caribbean, after
all.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:57.030
The films's $98 million budget was put to
good use.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:59.969
But critics pounced on the movie, and the
public stayed away in droves.

00:00:59.969 --> 00:01:04.250
Carolco was already pushed so far into the
red that the company filed for bankruptcy

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:05.550
in November 1995.

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:09.380
Cutthroat Island's paltry $10 million box
office take following its release a month

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:11.050
later didn't help.

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:12.290
Town &amp; Country

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:17.110
It was supposed to be just a standard romantic
comedy aimed at an older, upscale audience,

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:21.450
yet it took three years and the budget of
a summer sci-fi blockbuster to get Town &amp; Country

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:22.549
to theaters.

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:26.990
Warren Beatty stars as a wealthy, philandering
architect, married for 25 years to an interior

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:28.930
designer played by Diane Keaton.

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:32.570
The couple's friends, played by Garry Shandling
and Goldie Hawn, are caught up in their own

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:37.000
infidelity mess, and further relationship
hijinks ensue when the action switches from

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:38.310
town...to country.

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:42.549
The film's projected budget of $44 million
included 10 million for Beatty, who reportedly

00:01:42.549 --> 00:01:46.649
was such a perfectionist that his demand for
endless retakes stretched out the film's shoot

00:01:46.649 --> 00:01:47.880
for years.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:51.329
Several of the film's stars also weren't happy
with the screenplay, and brought in their

00:01:51.329 --> 00:01:54.689
own script doctors to punch it up while the
movie was in production.

00:01:54.689 --> 00:01:58.409
Total final cost of Town &amp; Country: $90 million.

00:01:58.409 --> 00:02:03.479
Total domestic box office take of Town &amp; Country,
upon its release in spring 2001: just under

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:04.480
7-million-dollars.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:06.560
"God, I wish I'd known that."

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:08.980
Revolution

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:11.430
On paper, it sounds like an undeniable hit.

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:16.610
Hugh Hudson, coming off the back-to-back critical
and commercial hits Chariots of Fire and Greystoke,

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:21.110
directed this epic Revolutionary War movie
as told through the eyes of Tom Dobb, a reluctant-to-fight

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:24.180
New York state fur trapper, played by Al Pacino.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:28.200
Oddly enough, this movie about the American
fight for freedom was produced by a British

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.900
studio and filmed in the English countryside.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:35.430
Still, $28 million was poured into Revolution,
making it one of most expensive movies of

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:38.540
1985, up there with Rambo: First Blood Part
II.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:43.860
But Revolution was a massive box office flop
— it earned just over 350-thousand-dollars

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:45.230
in the U.S.

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:47.420
The Adventures of Pluto Nash

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:51.849
Sometimes mixing two genres works wonders:
Lethal Weapon's action-comedy hybrid being

00:02:51.849 --> 00:02:53.170
a classic example.

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:56.940
But how about combining "gangster movie,"
"science fiction," and "comedy"?

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:57.940
Not so much.

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:02.510
Set in the year 2080, The Adventures of Pluto
Nash starred Eddie Murphy as an ex-smuggler

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:07.120
who gets out of prison and buys a mob-connected
nightclub on the moon, using some "Lunar States

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:08.120
of America" cash ...

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:09.580
"Why don't you take these 'Hillarys'?

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:10.799
We appreciate you helping us out."

00:03:10.799 --> 00:03:15.799
The screenplay bounced around Hollywood for
more than a decade before finally being produced

00:03:15.799 --> 00:03:17.190
in 2000 — and then it sat on the shelf.

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:21.750
Warner Bros. finally released it late in the
summer of 2002, only to see it pull in just

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:26.890
$4.4 million at the domestic box office, which
didn't even touch the film's staggering $100

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:28.450
million budget.

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:29.659
Lucky You

00:03:29.659 --> 00:03:35.379
The mid-2000s poker craze begat a few poker
movies, such as this 2007 drama about a second-generation

00:03:35.379 --> 00:03:38.150
card shark looking to compete in the World
Series of Poker.

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:42.319
Lucky You boasted an impressive lineup and
was directed by Academy Award nominee Curtis

00:03:42.319 --> 00:03:45.940
Hanson, who co-wrote the script with Academy
Award winner Eric Roth.

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:49.549
Perhaps it was the level of top-notch talent
that ran the budget of this low-key movie

00:03:49.549 --> 00:03:52.260
up to approximately $55 million.

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:56.739
But that roster didn't attract big audiences
— Lucky You pulled in just $5.7 million

00:03:56.739 --> 00:03:58.340
at the domestic box office.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:01.599
It was a lukewarm reception that certainly
wasn't helped by the negative reviews from

00:04:01.599 --> 00:04:05.930
critics like Rex Reed of the New York Observer,
who argued that it "doesn't have enough energy

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:09.620
to keep the most catatonic tournament-poker
addict awake."

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:10.620
Monkeybone

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:14.840
Well, it's certainly original: Monkeybone
is about a cartoonist played by Brendan Fraser

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:19.000
who, while in a coma, travels to a dark fantasy
land called Down Town.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:23.480
There, he must defeat both Death incarnate
and Monkeybone, an evil cartoon monkey of

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:24.790
his own creation.

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:29.000
Mixing live action and animation is a technically
demanding and expensive task, and director

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:34.070
Henry Selick, best known for The Nightmare
Before Christmas, ran up a tab of $75 million.

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:37.670
Executives relieved Selick of his directorial
duties after a terrible test screening, calling

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:39.300
for a brand new cut of the movie.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:43.780
Then executive producer Chris Columbus oversaw
another edit to make it more palatable.

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:47.630
All the while, the release date was changed
so often that the studio only had two weeks

00:04:47.630 --> 00:04:51.490
before its March 2001 opening to mount an
advertising campaign.

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:56.610
The end result for Monkeybone: domestic revenues
of just over $5 million.

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:57.610
Gigli

00:04:57.610 --> 00:05:00.110
It's a punchline in film history, synonymous
with "flop."

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:06.050
And for good reason: For starters, 2003's
Gigli has a title that isn't immediately pronounceable,

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:09.530
and a plot that falls somewhere between nonsensical
and offensive.

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:13.880
Ben Affleck plays a thug tasked by his criminal
bosses to intimidate a federal prosecutor

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:16.580
by kidnapping her developmentally disabled
brother.

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:20.390
Things don't go as planned, so they send in
Jennifer Lopez, another gangster, to help

00:05:20.390 --> 00:05:21.390
him.

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:23.970
Affleck winds up falling in love with her,
but it turns out that she's gay.

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:28.070
Nothing about Gigli seemed terribly promising
on paper, but it earned extra attention when

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:32.990
its stars wound up in a tabloid-ready relationship,
ultimately amplifying the negative pre-release

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:33.990
buzz.

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:37.750
When it arrived in theaters in August 2003,
critics destroyed it.

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:42.530
Against a reported $54 million budget, the
film took in a hair over $6 million and was

00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:45.040
pulled from theaters after just three weeks.

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:49.320
This deprived many filmgoers the opportunity
to hear Christopher Walken praise Marie Callender's

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:50.390
as only he can ...

00:05:50.390 --> 00:05:52.960
"Mmmm good.

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:54.360
Put some on your head!

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:58.090
Your tongue would slap your brains out trying
to get to it."

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:01.020
And now you've witnessed the only good to
have come out of Gigli.

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:02.150
You're welcome.

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:03.150
Thanks for watching!

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:05.560
